.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt NANOSLEEP 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nanosleep ,
.Nm clock_nanosleep
.Nd suspend the calling process for an interval or until a time
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In time.h
.Ft int
.Fn nanosleep "const struct timespec *req" "struct timespec *rem"
.Ft int
.Fn clock_nanosleep "clockid_t clockid" "int flags" "const struct timespec *req" "struct timespec *rem"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn nanosleep
suspends the calling process for the interval in
.Fa req .
The
.Va tv_sec
field specifies seconds and
.Va tv_nsec
specifies nanoseconds.
Seconds must be nonnegative; nanoseconds must be from 0 through 999999999.
.Pp
The kernel ends a nonzero interval at its end, as Linux's high-resolution
timers end one: the local APIC timer is armed to interrupt then when the
sleep begins, so a sleep that no signal ends is never shorter than asked,
up to the limit below, and longer only by the interrupt's latency.
The deadline tick is the first at or after the end, measured from where in
the tick in progress the call is; where the local APIC timer is not the
tick, or before a reference counter keeps the clock, the sleep lasts to
that tick, at most a tick longer than asked.
The process blocks meanwhile; other runnable processes continue to
execute.
The sleep also lasts until
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC ,
read at the call, has advanced by the interval: a tick the timer lost
while the processor could not take it leaves the tick count behind that
clock, which keeps its last reading, and the sleep then waits for the count
to catch up.
A zero relative interval blocks for 50 microseconds, Linux's default timer
slack, within which Linux's zero sleep ends, and ends between ticks as
above, giving other runnable threads time to execute; where there is no
interrupt between ticks, it blocks until the first tick after that.
.Pp
Once the deadline tick has come, and the clock has advanced by the
interval, the call returns 0 even if a signal is pending; a pending signal
that is not blocked is acted on as the call returns.
If a signal that is neither blocked nor ignored is pending when the
process runs before its deadline, the sleep ends.
When the signal runs a handler the call fails with
.Er EINTR ,
whatever the handler's flags; when it runs none, as a stop and a
continue, the sleep goes on through
.Xr restart_syscall 2
to the deadline fixed at the first call, as Linux's does, and returns 0
then, or at once when the deadline passed while the process was stopped.
A raw call given
.Fa rem
returns 0 instead, as Linux's does, when the remainder below comes to none
by the time the signal is seen.
Unless
.Fa rem
is null, the raw call stores there the time left until the clock reaches
the sleep's end, where the interrupt between ticks ends the sleep; where it
does not, the time left to the deadline tick, less how far into the tick in
progress the timer is, or, once that tick has come, the time left until the
clock has advanced by the interval.
A sleep begun again with the remainder ends where the first would have,
later by the time it takes to begin it.
.Ss clock_nanosleep
.Fn clock_nanosleep
sleeps against the clock
.Fa clockid .
With
.Fa flags
0,
.Fa req
is an interval, slept as
.Fn nanosleep
sleeps one, with the same deadline and the same remainder, except that
nothing is written to
.Fa rem
when the sleep reaches its deadline.
With
.Dv TIMER_ABSTIME
.Pq 1 ,
.Fa req
is a time of the clock: the call returns when the clock reads that time,
by the interrupt between ticks as above, or at the first tick at which it
reads that time or later where there is none, and at once for a time
already past.
A signal that runs no handler restarts such a sleep with its own
arguments, as Linux does, and one that runs a handler makes it fail with
.Er EINTR .
Real time and time since boot are read to the microsecond, so a time of
either that has passed within the tick in progress returns at once, though
its tick has not yet come.
.Fa rem
is not written, and other flag bits are ignored.
.Pp
Four clocks can be slept on.
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC
and
.Dv CLOCK_BOOTTIME
read the same time since boot, and
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME
is that time with the boot's real time added, so an absolute sleep on any
of the three ends when time since boot reaches the time asked for, less the
real-time clock's offset on that clock.
A setting of the real-time clock or a leap second moves an absolute sleep
on
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME ,
as in Linux: a step past its time ends it at the next tick, and a step back
keeps it sleeping until the clock reaches its time again; a step back after
the clock has reached its time leaves the sleep ended.
The fourth is the caller's process CPU clock,
.Dv CLOCK_PROCESS_CPUTIME_ID ,
or any process's in the negative form
.Xr clock_gettime 2
describes.
While the caller sleeps, its process's CPU clock advances by the time of
the process's other threads alone: it is read at every tick while another
thread of the process lives, as another process's CPU clock is, and the
sleep ends at the first tick that finds it at the time asked for.
With no other thread it does not advance, and a sleep on it for an
interval, or until a time not yet reached, ends only at a signal.
For an interval on a CPU clock a signal leaves the CPU time still due in
.Fa rem .
.Pp
The raw and coarse clocks,
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW ,
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME_COARSE
and
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC_COARSE ,
and the calling thread's CPU clock,
.Dv CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID ,
cannot be slept on, as on Linux.
.Ss Differences from Linux
Linux's remainder runs to its timer's latest expiry, the end and the
thread's timer slack, 50 microseconds by default; here it runs to the end,
where the sleep ends.
Where there is no interrupt between ticks, the remainder counts whole 1 ms
ticks, and a sleep begun again with it can end up to a tick early.
Linux can also sleep on
.Dv CLOCK_TAI
and its two alarm clocks, which this kernel does not have.
.Ss System call interface
The i386 system call number is 162 for
.Fn nanosleep ,
with EBX pointing to the request and ECX to the optional remainder, and
267 for
.Fn clock_nanosleep ,
with the clock in EBX, the flags in ECX, the request in EDX and the
remainder in ESI.
On x86-64 the numbers are 35 and 230, with the same arguments in RDI, RSI,
RDX and R10.
The kernel timespec consists of two signed fields, seconds, then
nanoseconds, of 32 bits on i386 and 64 on x86-64.
The kernel limits the seconds field to 2^40, about 34800 years, keeps the
nanoseconds and rounds the interval up to whole ticks; a sleep so cut ends
at its tick without the clock's check.
.Pp
The raw calls write the remainder only when a signal ends the sleep, as
Linux's do; a sleep that reaches its deadline leaves
.Fa rem
as it was.
A remainder that cannot be written then makes the call fail with
.Er EFAULT
in place of
.Er EINTR .
.Pp
musl converts its public timespec to the kernel's 32-bit structure; a
.Fa tv_sec
above 2147483647 is clamped before the call and the excess is added back
to the remainder on
.Er EINTR ,
so the public remainder need not equal the time left to the kernel's
deadline.
It copies the remainder back to the caller only on
.Er EINTR ;
on success, the caller's
.Fa rem
is unchanged.
.Pp
musl's
.Fn clock_nanosleep
calls
.Fn nanosleep
for an interval of
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME
and
.Fn clock_nanosleep
for everything else, and answers
.Er EINVAL
for
.Dv CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID
without calling the kernel.
glibc does the same for that clock, and its
.Fn nanosleep ,
.Fn sleep
and
.Fn usleep
are
.Fn clock_nanosleep
on
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME .
On i386 both first try system call 407, the 64-bit
.Fn clock_nanosleep ,
for a time past the 32-bit range, and the kernel answers
.Er ENOSYS .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn nanosleep
returns 0 on completion, or -1 on failure and sets
.Va errno .
.Fn clock_nanosleep
returns 0 on completion, or the error number itself on failure, without
setting
.Va errno .
The raw kernel calls return a negative error number on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/timeprobe.c
requests a 150 ms sleep through
.Fn nanosleep ,
system call 162 on i386 and 35 on x86-64, after forking a child.
It checks the elapsed interval and that the child made progress while
the parent slept.
.Pa tests/musl/clockidmusl.c
sleeps until a time of each clock that can be slept on and checks that the
clock reads it afterwards, has a signal end a relative and an absolute
sleep, checks the clocks and requests that are refused, and sleeps on the
process CPU clock while another thread of the process spins.
.Pa tests/restartprobe.c
stops and continues a sleep and checks that it ends at its first deadline
with its remainder written, and that a handler ends it with
.Er EINTR .
.Sh ERRORS
The kernel handlers return:
.Bl -tag -width "[EOPNOTSUPP]"
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The request is null or cannot be read, there is no current process,
or a signal ended the sleep and the remainder cannot be written.
The musl wrapper reads the public request and writes the public
remainder in user space.
.It Bq Er EINTR
A signal that ran a handler interrupted the sleep.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
Seconds are negative, or nanoseconds are outside the permitted range.
For
.Fn clock_nanosleep ,
the clock is 8 or greater, a thread CPU clock named by ID, or a CPU clock
of no process that has not been reaped.
A clock of 8 or greater is refused before the request is read, the others
after it.
.It Bq Er EOPNOTSUPP
.Fn clock_nanosleep
was asked to sleep on
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW ,
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME_COARSE ,
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC_COARSE
or
.Dv CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr clock_gettime 2 ,
.Xr gettimeofday 2 ,
.Xr sigaction 2
